As mentioned in theErrors chapter, different types of errors can occur while running a program - such as coding mistakes, invalid input, or unexpected situations.

When an error occurs, Java will normally stop and generate an error message. The technical term for this is: Java will throw anexception(throw an error).

Exception handling lets you catch and handle errors during runtime - so your program doesn't crash.

It uses different keywords:

Thetrystatement allows you to define a block of code to be 
tested for errors while it is being executed.

Thecatchstatement allows you to define a block of code to 
be executed, if an error occurs in the try block.

